ime  and never been  more need for nevada to have its voice  heard on capitol hill so the vegas  chamber is taking the largest delegation  ever to washington dc we have more than  200 people going and it's really an  opportunity just for all of us to not  only talk with our local nevada  delegation but also congressional  members from across the country and to  build camaraderie among the business  community here what are some of the big  issues we've talked about so many times  with the business community in las vegas  that you think lawmakers in washington  need to know about well there are  several  not the least of which is water we've  been talking a lot about water lately  conservation you know there are seven  states uh in the colorado river  commission that are impacted the water  it not only provides water to millions  of households across the west but also  hydro power hydroelectric power and so  forth and so that's a topic that we  really need to discuss with our nevada  congressional delegation and really  explain to them not that they don't  already know but it really  gives us a louder voice when we have so  many people to talk about how water  truly impacts not only our daily lives  but economic development how does it  affect economic development when if  you're a business that's looking for to  to set up shop somewhere maybe you've  been someplace else or starting from  scratch  the impact of water has to play into the  decisions that are made the relationship  with the chamber and whether las vegas  even looks appealing to that potential  business creator absolutely water is  definitely a factor as businesses are  looking to relocate here to southern  nevada we we have to face it we are in a  drying climate it's not just a drought  it is a drying climate and and that's  okay it's workable but we have to know  how to  what are some of the best solutions and  ways to go about doing business in a  place that is a drying climate there are  obviously things that we can't do that  you would do in a in a wetter climate  another a long time issue for the vegas  ship that you and i have talked about  many times i-11  i-15 as well but especially i-11 both  interstates are important certainly to  las vegas where do things stand with  trying to increase development increase  funding to try and expand i 11 first and  foremost and maybe widen i-15 absolutely  you know we have gotten to a point  nevada is complete has completed our  portion of i-11  to arizona as you know phoenix and las  vegas are the only two major  metropolitan cities that are not  connected by a major freeway and so  we're working with our friends in  arizona  department of transportation to continue  that project and then for i-15 as you  know you can drive pick a holiday  weekend and it's kind of a parking lot  so  it's really important that we're able  we've been working with the lv cva as  well to to widen i-15 so that's on at  the top of our list as well  and i'll have more with the vegas  chamber next week on good day las vegas  next on politics now though what was in  those documents the latest on the  investigation into classified material  found at mar-a-lago during a raid by the  fbi plus why the aclu is suing the city  of las vegas over festivus this is 8  news  recovery  [Music]  you're watching politics now  [Music]  the nevada aclu is now suing to stop  curfews in downtown las vegas the curfew  from the city of las vegas and fremont  street experience restricts access for  anyone under 21 on weekend nights  fremont street experience right now with  the way they're doing this there's a  special use permit it has it was granted  in july for an event called festivus  that's to justify the curfew well it's  in effect through november but nothing's  really going on our the aclu argues  there is no such event it believes  fremont street is a public square for  first amendment purposes and 18 year  olds should be allowed  the justice department and president  donald trump they have to submit their  choices for an independent party to  review documents seized from mar-a-lago  a month ago the new report that says one  document that was found there at  mar-a-lago is about nuclear intelligence  the washington post also says some  documents found were so closely guarded  that many senior national security  officials didn't have access to them  themselves  a federal judge effectively halted parts  of the justice department's  investigation into former president  trump's handling of classified documents  now the justice department is asking the  judge to change that ruling says it  could do harm to the government and the  public alex limon joining us now from  washington to explain all of this alex  what's the doj's complaint here  well the issue here is that with that  ruling the judge like you said has  effectively halted the doj's  investigation because by ruling that a  special master should be appointed that  means that the doj essentially loses  access to all of the documents that were  seized from mar-a-lago because what has  to happen now is that that special  master needs time to go through all of  the documents and then decide whether  any of them fall under client attorney  privilege and if they do then those are  documents that the doj wouldn't have  access to and so what the doj is arguing  here is that this  time delay you know it could take some  time so many documents were seized it  could jeopardize the entire  investigation and so the doj has gone  back to the judge to ask  for that judge to potentially change  parts of this ruling  the doj says it's conducting a two-part  investigation on this can you tell us  what are those two different parts and  how are they impacted by this ruling  right so the first part of the doj  investigation is directly investigating  whether trump and his team broke any  federal laws by removing these documents  and moving them to mar-a-lago keep in  mind the doj has